# Error Message & Error Summary

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

When a form cannot accept an answer, people need to know three things: that something went wrong, which answer it is, and how to fix it. Civica gives this two parts that work together. An error message sits beside the field that needs correcting. An error summary sits at the start of the form and links to every message.

These are Civica patterns, not Home Assistant elements. Use them on Civica pages and in forms built with Civica form markup. Home Assistant draws its own errors in its own forms and dialogs, and a theme can set only their colour, `error-color`. For the alert Home Assistant uses inside its dialogs, see [Alert & Tip Callout](alert-and-tip-callout.html).

This page covers:

- Error Message & Error Summary
  - Civica Error Message Pattern
  - Civica Error Summary Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An error message is a line of text between the field’s label and hint and the field itself. It starts with the word “Error:”, which is hidden from view but available to assistive technology, followed by the correction in plain words. The field has a red border and `aria-invalid="true"`, and points to both the hint and the error with `aria-describedby`.

An error summary is a box with the title “There is a problem” and a list of links, one for each error. Each link takes people to the field that has the same message beside it.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-error-message-pattern"><code>Civica Error Message Pattern</code></h3>

Use it after an attempted save or next step. Put the message with the affected field and repeat it in the error summary. Describe the correction, including units or permitted choices when they help.

Do not check fields as people tab through them before they have answered. An offline device or a missing permission is not a mistake in the person’s answer, so explain that condition separately.

For Home Assistant settings:

- Tell a mistake in the answer apart from a service failure. Keep a correct room name if saving fails because the connection was lost.
- Do not invent numeric ranges. Use the integration’s declared minimum and maximum and name their unit.
- Keep the label and the hint linked to the field when you add the message.
- The red border supports the words and never replaces them.

The error colour is `error-color`, #CA3535 in light mode and #F0C6C6 in dark mode.

<h3 id="child-civica-error-summary-pattern"><code>Civica Error Summary Pattern</code></h3>

Use the summary even when only one field needs correcting. Put it at the start of the form content, below any back link, and use the same words as the messages beside the fields.

Do not use it for a successful save, a general notice or an alert about a physical device. Do not show it together with an unrelated success banner, because the two compete.

After a failed Home Assistant settings save:

- Move focus to the summary once, and keep every answer that is not secret.
- When the form owns its page, start the page title with “Error:”.
- In an existing dialog, keep focus inside the dialog and use the dialog’s own accessible title. Do not replace the application title.
- Link each message to the control it is about. For a date made of several inputs, link to the first incorrect input. For radios, link to the first option.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The summary and the messages use the same words, so people can find an error from either.
- Each message is tied to its field in code, not only by position or colour.
- Both patterns are plain markup, so they do not depend on Home Assistant. They do not add checking logic: the form must still decide what is wrong and when to say so.
- Home Assistant’s own forms do not use this markup, so the look differs there.
- A long list of errors is hard to work through. Ask fewer questions in one step.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Write each message as a correction, in plain words, using the field’s own name: “Enter a room name”, not “Invalid input”. Say what to do, and give the unit and the choices where they help. Keep what people typed so they can change it, and do not ask for it again. Do not lock the field against pasting.

Success criteria that apply:

- 3.3.1 Error Identification and 3.3.3 Error Suggestion: name the field and say how to fix it.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: keep answers that were correct.
- 3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (Minimum): do not block pasting into a field.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: the summary appears once after a failed save. Do not show it again while people type.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the words carry the meaning, and the red border supports them.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: test the message and the border in your own theme, in light and dark modes.
- 2.4.2 Page Titled and 2.4.3 Focus Order: the page title starts with “Error:” and focus moves to the summary once.
- 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): test that the focused summary and field are not hidden by other parts of the page.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose: keep the label and hint tied to the field.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: test the messages at 320 CSS pixels.

Test with the [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html) checks, in light and dark modes. Click every summary link. Submit with the keyboard. Correct only one of several errors and check the summary. Retry after a connection failure. Check that errors are not announced again while people type.

